course: dictionary
lesson: 6644
title: The nucleus, the centre
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: The nucleus is the centre of something, often the part that holds the most important things together. «An atom is made of smaller parts: protons and neutrons in a tiny middle called the nucleus, and electrons around it.» The clue is «tiny middle», so the nucleus is the centre of the atom.
explain: In biology, the nucleus is the centre of a cell. «Inside each cell's nucleus is DNA, a long instruction book that tells the cell what to do.» The words «tells the cell what to do» show that the nucleus controls the cell.
explain: The word also works in everyday talk. A few key players form the nucleus of a team, which means they are the central, most important part of the team. To check your meaning, swap in «centre» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Nucleus means the centre, or the most important central part, of something.
fact: The plural of nucleus is nuclei.
fact: Plant and animal cells have a nucleus, but bacteria do not.
fact: The nucleus of an atom holds almost all of its mass, with electrons around it.
fact: «Stretched out, the DNA in one human cell is about two metres long, packed tightly into the tiny nucleus.»
exercise: In «an atom is made of smaller parts», where is the nucleus? | Between the electrons and the walls | Floating outside the atom | *In the tiny middle of the atom
exercise: Which part of a cell holds its DNA and controls it? | The Sun's core | The microscope | *The nucleus
exercise: A few key players form the nucleus of a team. What does nucleus mean here? | The coach's car | *The central, most important part | The noisy supporters
test: In «the nucleus of the cell», what does nucleus mean? | *Centre | Edge | Wall | Tail
test: How do you write more than one nucleus? | nucleo | nucleuses | *nuclei | nucleis
exam: Which living thing does not have a nucleus in its cells? | Animal cells | *Bacteria | Plant cells
